WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.810
The probe used multiple SQL injections,

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.745
but I've yet to find
any compromised files.

00:00:04.745 --> 00:00:06.270
This is terrible!

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.970
When I first saw this show,

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.960
I shut down my TV that very second.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.810
Hi, I'm Keren Elazari,

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.440
a security researcher,
author, and friendly hacker.

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.050
I've been in the cybersecurity space

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:20.200
for more than 25 years.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.340
I'm the founder of Israel's
largest hacker community,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:26.500
BSidesTLV, and the Leading Cyber Ladies.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.058
Today, we're gonna take a look at

00:00:28.058 --> 00:00:31.533
some cool hacking clips
from film and television

00:00:31.533 --> 00:00:34.136
and discuss, how real is it?

00:00:40.214 --> 00:00:42.950
Yikes! I wouldn't do that.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:46.258
That is bad, bad OPSEC.

00:00:46.258 --> 00:00:48.008
Bad operational security.

00:00:48.008 --> 00:00:51.020
The computer might actually
be physically booby-trapped.

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:52.680
If I had the computer like that,

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.550
I was bringing it in for investigation,

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.800
I might even x-ray it before
I did anything with it.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.300
When we bring in a device

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.880
for a digital forensics investigation,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.950
we usually open it up
in a lab environment,

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:08.261
not in the middle of our agency office.

00:01:11.388 --> 00:01:12.445
It's his omega site.

00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:14.360
I don't know the term "omega site."

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.670
I think that's just added Hollywood jargon

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:17.793
to make it look scary.

00:01:19.225 --> 00:01:20.783
Most encrypted level he has.

00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:21.900
It wouldn't be a level.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:24.540
It might be a file or, you know,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.960
a segment of the hard drive.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:28.610
There's no levels.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:30.130
It doesn't look like this.

00:01:30.130 --> 00:01:32.680
There's definitely no 3D, you know,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.500
moving animations of
what the file is doing.

00:01:36.875 --> 00:01:40.120
Q: Looks like obfuscated code
to conceal its true purpose.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.500
OK, that bit is real.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:44.050
Yes, you could obfuscate your code,

00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:46.691
and malware authors often do this.

00:01:46.691 --> 00:01:49.328
Q: He's using a polymorphic
engine to mutate the code.

00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:51.110
Whenever I try to gain access, it changes.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.851
We have seen polymorphic code,

00:01:53.851 --> 00:01:55.642
specifically with malware.

00:01:55.642 --> 00:01:59.440
So, now we finally see a
hex version of the code.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:02.726
Hex stands for hexadecimal.

00:02:02.726 --> 00:02:05.998
Now, hexadecimal means 16.

00:02:05.998 --> 00:02:08.930
You typically can see only 16 characters.

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:11.890
0 till 9, A to F.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:14.030
You won't see G.

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:15.410
You won't see R.

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:16.747
You won't see O.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.380
You can do a lot of things with code,

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.930
and it can be beautiful,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.860
but it's not gonna look like a map.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.450
It's not gonna have these,
you know, curly lines.

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.940
This is something completely different.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.870
I think this is maybe three out of 10,

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.793
and I'm just giving them
some points for, you know,

00:02:36.793 --> 00:02:38.862
putting in some actual, real-world terms,

00:02:38.862 --> 00:02:43.770
like malware, polymorphism,
and code obfuscation,

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.150
but that's pretty much
where the realism ends,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:47.683
unfortunately, Mr. Bond.

00:02:49.427 --> 00:02:50.560
Darlene: They're in the
middle of the final round

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.310
of the qualifier for a CTF.

00:02:52.310 --> 00:02:55.412
So, Elliot and Darlene are
visiting a hacker space

00:02:55.412 --> 00:02:58.475
that is currently hosting a CTF.

00:02:58.475 --> 00:03:01.560
A CTF is a capture-the-flag competition,

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.070
and this is a type of hacker game.

00:03:04.070 --> 00:03:05.890
It might be about decrypting

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.750
a really unique piece of
code or something like that.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:09.591
So, in this scene,

00:03:09.591 --> 00:03:12.265
they're in the qualifying
rounds for DEF CON,

00:03:12.265 --> 00:03:15.540
and DEF CON is a real-world
hacking conference.

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:17.440
It's the world's largest
hacking conference.

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.750
I've been to CTFs at convention centers,

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.550
university campuses.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:26.360
There's even hackathons

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:29.670
and CTFs that take place in Italian farms.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.152
Although the one they're at
right now is extra-underground.

00:03:31.152 --> 00:03:32.927
Elliot: They let you
save and load your game,

00:03:32.927 --> 00:03:34.343
restoring all the mines you found

00:03:34.343 --> 00:03:36.234
and all the shells you cleared.

00:03:36.234 --> 00:03:37.330
That's the weakness.

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:40.540
What Elliot is doing could be
even considered quite rude,

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.300
having somebody
shoulder-surf you like that.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:44.440
Yes, it does happen.

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.070
However, for Elliot to,
you know, within one second

00:03:48.070 --> 00:03:50.740
understand what's going on that screen,

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.230
get all the context of that code,

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.730
and then tell them how
to win that challenge,

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.580
I mean, I understand the show, of course,

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.420
sets him up to be of
above-average intelligence,

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:02.140
but he would have to be
like a supercomputer.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:04.000
Elliot: The game trusts
whatever data you give it

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:05.823
to recreate the board.

00:04:05.823 --> 00:04:06.850
Poison the data.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:08.720
You can make it run
whatever code you want.

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:12.140
It sounds like the hackers
need to reverse engineer

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:16.090
or to take apart a Minesweeper-style game.

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.870
This sounds quite realistic.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:18.990
It's actually based

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:22.850
on the real-world CTF challenge from 2012.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.450
So I think it's really cool
the show went to the trouble

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:27.783
of getting a real-world hacking challenge.

00:04:27.783 --> 00:04:29.703
[crowd cheering]

00:04:29.703 --> 00:04:32.500
A CTF room can get loud,

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.570
it can get sweaty, it can get smelly.

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:36.924
It can be electric.

00:04:36.924 --> 00:04:38.650
Elliot: All I have to
do is hack the registrar

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:40.510
and change the name server configs.

00:04:41.686 --> 00:04:44.320
What he's doing right
now is very realistic,

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:47.970
and it would have taken him
a lot longer than it did.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:52.380
One does not hack a
registrar in two seconds.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.410
Elliot needs to get in touch

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.560
with the backdoor that they planted

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:59.460
inside E Corp in the past season.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.810
In order to do that,

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:03.410
he's utilizing the fact that backdoor,

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.120
which is basically a computer software,

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.490
has a hard-coded C2 domain.

00:05:09.490 --> 00:05:13.180
C2 in this context means
command and control.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.930
And it's oftentimes where hackers

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:19.160
or malicious criminals
create a piece of code

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.910
that's going to run inside
an organizational network,

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.605
but in order to be in a position

00:05:24.605 --> 00:05:26.558
to communicate with the backdoor,

00:05:26.558 --> 00:05:28.830
Elliot first needs to
take over the domain.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.180
And the next thing he does,

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:31.321
and we see him do it,

00:05:31.321 --> 00:05:33.337
is issue the command "shred."

00:05:33.337 --> 00:05:37.010
Shred is a Unix command
to not just delete files,

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:40.320
but also rewrite them so
that the files would be

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.292
much more difficult to recover,

00:05:42.292 --> 00:05:44.890
even with specialized forensic software.

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:48.220
I'm gonna rate this
scene at nine out of 10.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.692
I'm only deducting the one point

00:05:50.692 --> 00:05:53.350
just because of how
fast everything happened

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:55.350
and how quickly those hackers

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.973
let Elliot jump into their CTF game.

00:06:02.225 --> 00:06:03.720
Well, we can tell she's a hacker.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.866
She's got all those
stickers on her laptop.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:12.760
Nine Ball, Rihanna, is using
open-source intelligence,

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:15.050
which is a fancy term for the internet.

00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:18.440
This is what's called a
spear-phishing attack.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.850
She's not just going on
a phishing expedition;

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.160
she is spear phishing.

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.542
She's targeting this particular individual

00:06:24.542 --> 00:06:26.333
with an email with a topic,

00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:28.649
something that he's
really passionate about.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.170
There are definitely attacks out there

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:34.700
that would give an attacker
control of your webcam,

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.545
and they could even turn on your webcam

00:06:36.545 --> 00:06:38.170
and you wouldn't know that it's on.

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:41.020
But it typically takes
a little bit more time

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.290
and a tiny little bit more interaction

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:45.170
on the side of the victim.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.520
They would maybe run an
application, install something.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.395
It will take a little bit extra.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.715
These types of physical boxes

00:06:58.715 --> 00:07:02.298
that would allow you
to unlock any passwords

00:07:02.298 --> 00:07:05.480
don't really exist for computer passwords.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.220
Many of you have a phone that

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:09.610
if you put in the four-digit PIN

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:11.670
or a seven-digit PIN and you get it wrong,

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:16.110
the phone will get wiped after
five or 10 wrong attempts.

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.148
So those physical boxes in the real world,

00:07:18.148 --> 00:07:20.066
used mostly by law enforcement,

00:07:20.066 --> 00:07:23.640
would bypass that using all
kinds of different tricks,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:25.752
but they would require physical access

00:07:25.752 --> 00:07:27.393
to be connected with a cable

00:07:27.393 --> 00:07:29.326
to their target device, to the phone.

00:07:29.326 --> 00:07:32.993
It makes sense that they
require an additional password

00:07:32.993 --> 00:07:35.590
just to get access to
that particular software.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:37.298
That's cool. That makes sense.

00:07:37.298 --> 00:07:41.170
However, this looks like
a 12-character password,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:43.810
and it's got not just
numbers, but also upper case,

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.495
lower case, and special characters.

00:07:45.495 --> 00:07:47.912
For a password of that length,

00:07:47.912 --> 00:07:52.786
it would have about 94 to the power of 12

00:07:52.786 --> 00:07:54.735
different possible combinations.

00:07:54.735 --> 00:07:58.080
That's a number so big that
I can't even spell it out.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.384
You'd have to use the whole screen

00:07:59.384 --> 00:08:01.030
just to write it down, right here.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:04.480
There is no way an electronic
box could crack that.

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:06.662
And if Nine Ball has a box like that,

00:08:06.662 --> 00:08:08.104
it's more valuable than whatever it is

00:08:08.104 --> 00:08:10.480
they're gonna steal from
The Metropolitan Museum.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:12.520
I would probably say the first part,

00:08:12.520 --> 00:08:15.130
about the spear phishing,
is extremely realistic,

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.840
but then it kind of loses the realness.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:22.823
So I'm gonna average
it at seven out of 10.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.087
I think it was the turning point,

00:08:29.087 --> 00:08:32.370
where Hollywood started
showing realistic hacking.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:33.703
So, what we're seeing here is that

00:08:33.703 --> 00:08:35.253
Trinity is using Nmap,

00:08:35.253 --> 00:08:39.148
which is a legit network
scanning and mapping tool

00:08:39.148 --> 00:08:41.403
that hackers use all the time.

00:08:43.790 --> 00:08:47.270
We also see her using
something called SSH Nuke.

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.860
So, SSH Nuke refers to "secure shell."

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.899
And SSH Nuke is, according to the movie,

00:08:52.899 --> 00:08:56.573
basically an attack
against the SSH service,

00:08:56.573 --> 00:08:59.830
where she's taking advantage
of a specific vulnerability.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:00.910
And it's even telling us

00:09:00.910 --> 00:09:05.180
it's attempting to exploit
SSH version one, CRC 32.

00:09:05.180 --> 00:09:07.621
So, this was a real-world

00:09:07.621 --> 00:09:10.421
vulnerability in SSH

00:09:10.421 --> 00:09:12.247
that was only discovered

00:09:12.247 --> 00:09:16.310
maybe about a month before
this film went to set.

00:09:16.310 --> 00:09:19.230
So as somebody was working
on the screenwrite,

00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:20.920
as they were in preproduction,

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:24.510
this vulnerability in that
piece of code was discovered,

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:26.550
and they already featured it in the movie,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:29.533
which I think is extremely
timely, extremely accurate.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:38.010
The only tiny element here
that isn't that realistic

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.350
is that she is resetting the password.

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:43.010
If she successfully
exploits that vulnerability,

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:45.700
the exploit would give
her root privileges.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:48.500
She wouldn't necessarily
have to reset the password.

00:09:49.609 --> 00:09:51.520
There is no way she could hack like that

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:53.280
and not make a ton of typos.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:54.230
All the hackers know

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:56.680
you need fingerless gloves to type fast.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:10:01.593
I'm gonna rate this
scene at 9.5 out of 10.

00:10:01.593 --> 00:10:03.833
And I'm taking away half a point

00:10:03.833 --> 00:10:05.257
just because of the gloves, girl.

00:10:06.973 --> 00:10:08.889
[keypad beeping]

00:10:08.889 --> 00:10:11.040
[door buzzing]

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:13.870
So, Lisbeth here is doing the groundwork.

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:15.630
She is casing her target.

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.640
What she's doing is basically hanging out

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:22.493
and catching, hearing the code

00:10:22.493 --> 00:10:25.720
as it's being touched in the keypad.

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.380
And it sounded like one, two, one, two,

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:28.810
which looks like what she's pressing.

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:30.085
So, in the real world,

00:10:30.085 --> 00:10:32.260
it's not that difficult to understand

00:10:32.260 --> 00:10:33.990
what the different digits sound like

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:35.630
if you train your ear to it.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:36.880
She's not gonna mess around with it.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:38.490
She's not gonna plug her computer

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:41.360
and be seen doing all
kinds of nefarious things.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.388
She's gonna take photos, get out of there,

00:10:43.388 --> 00:10:45.120
and then analyze those photos

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.000
to see exactly what the hardware setup is,

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.373
what the router is,

00:10:49.373 --> 00:10:51.481
what the type of communication setup is

00:10:51.481 --> 00:10:52.770
in that apartment building.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:56.140
And then she procures a specific device

00:10:56.140 --> 00:10:59.100
from one of her fellow hackers.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.960
I think that's very realistic.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.010
Looks like a, you know, a hacker space.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:05.768
Looks like a lot of places
I've visited, definitely.

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.930
The device itself, it's hard
to say exactly what it is.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.280
We see that it's a Nokia.

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:14.810
This might be in reference

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:17.970
to something called the Nokia N900,

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:22.020
which used to be known in
hacker circles as the pwn phone.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:24.480
And it was a phone that was used primarily

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:26.530
for wireless-network hacking.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:28.650
However, it didn't look exactly like this,

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:30.820
so this might be a specialized device.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:32.642
It might be a tablet that's got

00:11:32.642 --> 00:11:36.930
both a cellular connection
and a network interface.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.780
It might be something similar to this.

00:11:38.780 --> 00:11:40.265
The device she's using in the movie

00:11:40.265 --> 00:11:41.540
is a little bit dramatized.

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:45.140
It's a device that you can
plug into the ethernet.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:47.532
That's where you put in
the network connection.

00:11:47.532 --> 00:11:50.250
And it also has room for a SIM card.

00:11:50.250 --> 00:11:51.420
So, you put in a SIM card,

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:53.128
it's got a cellular uplink,

00:11:53.128 --> 00:11:54.669
and you plug it into the wall.

00:11:54.669 --> 00:11:57.140
It's actually designed to
look like an air freshener.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:11:59.120
And if I was doing a security assessment,

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:01.940
I would sneak in, just like Lisbeth does,

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:04.970
I would plug this into the
electricity, into the network,

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.860
and then I would use this other connection

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.210
to, basically, from my
remote hacker hideout,

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:14.140
to plug in, call up this bad boy,

00:12:14.140 --> 00:12:16.280
and run some network assessments,

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:17.330
see what I can sniff,

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.740
see what I can capture on that network.

00:12:19.740 --> 00:12:21.370
So, based on what we just saw,

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.194
I'm gonna give it 10 out of 10.

