Cipher already has everything set up,

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:37.223
all she has to do is click on a tablet.

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.510
I have definitely done that in the past.

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:49.390
So, looking for a note,
looking for a piece of paper

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:51.240
that's gonna have the password

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:53.510
for the system that you want to access.

00:16:53.510 --> 00:16:54.960
People write down their passwords,

00:16:54.960 --> 00:16:57.026
but even further than that,

00:16:57.026 --> 00:16:59.017
people recycle their passwords.

00:16:59.017 --> 00:17:00.550
I just have to go to some of the

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:03.775
many databases of leaked passwords,

00:17:03.775 --> 00:17:06.502
and I can easily find that you had

00:17:06.502 --> 00:17:08.935
five or six different accounts

00:17:08.935 --> 00:17:10.810
where you used the same password,

00:17:10.810 --> 00:17:12.428
so I'm gonna bet that you're gonna use

00:17:12.428 --> 00:17:15.570
that same password or a
very close variation of it

00:17:15.570 --> 00:17:18.064
for a lot of your other online services.

00:17:18.064 --> 00:17:20.260
And hackers do this all the time.

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:22.310
It's called credential stuffing,

00:17:22.310 --> 00:17:23.670
and we're just going to stuff them

00:17:23.670 --> 00:17:28.490
and try and see if any account,
any system lets us log in.

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:30.497
It's super easy for hackers

00:17:30.497 --> 00:17:33.440
to look over your social-media posts,

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.802
or maybe even when you're on a Zoom call,

00:17:35.802 --> 00:17:37.290
and you've got that Post-it

00:17:37.290 --> 00:17:39.840
with a password just behind you there.

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:41.160
So you want to enable things

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:44.880
like two-factor or
multifactor authentication.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.331
And sometimes that
additional layer of security

00:17:47.331 --> 00:17:49.780
